<h1>How Custom Tattoo Designs Work, Step by Step</h1>
<p class="muted">From your idea to finished, tattoo-ready artwork: the brief, the deposit, the drawing, the files, and the license.</p>

<p>A premade design is fast, but some tattoos exist only in your head — a memorial piece, a family symbol, your dog's portrait, a design built to fit one exact spot on your body. That is what custom design is for. On Tattoo Art Customs, custom pieces are drawn to order by design artists in the marketplace, and the process is deliberately simple: you describe it, pay a deposit, and receive finished artwork drawn within 48 hours of the paid deposit, with the balance due on delivery. This guide walks through every step so there are no surprises, including how to write a brief that gets you the drawing you actually want, and the faster routes — by-request and doodle-to-tattoo — for looser ideas.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Write the brief</h2>
<p>The brief is the difference between a good custom experience and a disappointing one. The artist can only draw what you describe, so spend ten real minutes on it. Cover five things. <strong>Subject:</strong> what is the piece, exactly — "a koi fish swimming upward, wrapped around a lotus" beats "something Japanese." <strong>Style:</strong> name one of the established styles (traditional, neo-traditional, realism, fine line, blackwork) or attach reference images of tattoos whose feel you want. <strong>Placement and size:</strong> where it goes and roughly how big, in inches — this decides how much detail the drawing can hold (see our <a href="/guides/choosing-a-design">choosing guide</a> for why size matters). <strong>Meaning and must-haves:</strong> the elements that carry the meaning, and anything that must not appear. <strong>Text:</strong> any lettering, spelled out letter by letter, with the font style you like — lettering typos are the most preventable disaster in tattooing, and the check is yours. Reference photos help enormously for portraits and pet pieces: send the clearest, highest-resolution shots you have.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Order and deposit</h2>
<p>Place the order on the <a href="/orders/custom">custom order page</a>. Custom designs are a flat price; half is due up front as the deposit and the other half on delivery, so neither side carries all the risk. If you catch the Saturday sale window, the custom price drops — the sale terms are listed on the site. Rush options, when available, appear on the order page for pieces you need sooner than the standard window. Once the deposit is paid, the clock starts: delivery is within 48 hours of the paid deposit.</p>

<h2>Step 3: The drawing</h2>
<p>Your artist draws the piece from your brief — not a filter, not a stock image with your name on it, an original drawing. Because the artists here design for skin, the artwork is built the way tattoos need: clean closed linework, shapes that read at the size you chose, and contrast that will survive healing. When the piece is done you receive the finished artwork to review. If something in the delivered drawing genuinely misses the brief — the wrong element, the wrong orientation — say so plainly and specifically; clear feedback is what makes any revision quick. Vague feedback ("make it pop") helps nobody, on any platform, with any artist.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Your files and your license</h2>
<p>Delivery is digital: you receive the full-color artwork and the linework version. The linework file is the one your tattoo artist turns into a stencil, so keep both files safe — they are your purchase. Custom designs carry the same single-use tattoo license as the rest of the marketplace: the artwork is yours to have tattooed once, and copyright remains with the artist, so it cannot be resold or printed on merchandise. All sales on the site are final, as stated in our <a href="/terms">terms</a> — which is exactly why the brief in step 1 matters so much. Take the files to any tattoo artist you trust: a verified shop from our <a href="/guides/for-tattoo-shops">shop network</a> or your own local artist. Artists work from clean linework every day; there is nothing unusual to explain.</p>

<h2>Two other routes to original art</h2>
<p><strong>By-request:</strong> if your idea is loose — a theme, a character, a vibe — post it on the <a href="/by-request">by-request board</a> instead of ordering a full custom. Designers browse the board, claim requests they want to draw, and you buy the finished design if it lands. It is slower and less controlled than a custom order, but it is how some of the most surprising pieces in the gallery started life. <strong>Doodle-to-tattoo:</strong> already have a sketch — yours, your kid's, a napkin drawing you love? <a href="/doodle-to-tattoo">Doodle-to-tattoo</a> turns a rough sketch into clean, tattoo-ready artwork: your lines, professionally redrawn with proper linework and shading. It is the fastest route of the three because the composition is already yours.</p>

<h2>Before you order: a quick checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Subject, style, placement, and size written down in the brief</li>
  <li>Reference images attached (the clearest you have)</li>
  <li>Any lettering double-checked, character by character</li>
  <li>Deposit ready — the 48-hour clock starts when it lands</li>
  <li>A tattoo artist in mind for the application, or a plan to find one</li>
  <li>Our <a href="/guides/aftercare">aftercare guide</a> read, so the appointment is not the last step you plan</li>
</ul>

<p>That is the whole process: brief, deposit, drawing, files, tattoo. When you are ready, <a href="/orders/custom">start a custom order</a> — or browse the <a href="/gallery">premade gallery</a> first, because sometimes the perfect design already exists and is ready today.</p>
